Subject: [PATCH] new rpg: WANDER_RANDOM is leashed to where it started

WHY THIS PATCH EXISTS

The family (Grug, Ugga, Grog, Bork, Og) walked out of their leveling zone and
into Burning Steppes - a high-level zone - while every one of them was still
under level 20. Grug spawned alone there after a death and "ran straight to a
?? leveled dragonkin and died." Nothing sent them there: no quest, no travel
order, no GM. They walked there under RPG_WANDER_RANDOM.

`NewRpgWanderRandomAction::Execute()` (NewRpgAction.cpp:363-369 at this pin)
does exactly two things: check for a quest giver, then

    return MoveRandomNear();

`NewRpgBaseAction::MoveRandomNear()` (NewRpgBaseAction.cpp:233) takes a
`center` parameter but the parameter is UNNAMED in the definition - it is
never read anywhere in the function body. Every candidate step is instead
built from the bot's own LIVE position:

    const float x = bot->GetPositionX();
    const float y = bot->GetPositionY();
    ...
    float dx = x + distance * cos(angle);
    float dy = y + distance * sin(angle);

The retry loop (8 attempts) only rejects a candidate for pathfinding failure,
unreachable geometry, or water - never for distance from anywhere. So each
individual step is short (moveStep is 8-15y at every call site in
NewRpgAction.cpp), but the walk has no memory of where it began. There is
nothing - no radius, no zone check, no timer - that stops the accumulated
walk from crossing a zone boundary over many ticks. That is a classic
unbounded random walk, and it is exactly how a sub-20 family ends up standing
next to an elite dragonkin in a level-50+ zone.

CONFIRMED: `center` IS EFFECTIVELY A NO-OP TODAY, NOT JUST AT THE WANDER-RANDOM
CALL SITE. Grepping every call to `MoveRandomNear` at this pin
(8d9f6aa6bc6d45f9ae0ee0675b9b1f8aa6937312):

  * NewRpgAction.cpp:342,357,417,513,564,607 and NewRpgAction.cpp:368
    (WANDER_RANDOM) never pass a third argument at all - center defaults null.
  * NewRpgOutdoorPvP.cpp:130 is the ONLY call site that passes one
    (`objectiveGO`, a capture point WorldObject*) - and it is discarded by the
    same unnamed-parameter body as everyone else.

So `center` was already inert everywhere it is used; this patch does not
disable behaviour anyone was relying on.

WHY THIS DOES NOT SIMPLY GIVE `center` A NAME AND READ IT

`center` is typed `WorldObject*` - a live pointer to an in-world object, which
is the right shape for NewRpgOutdoorPvP's use (patrol around a capture point
that exists as an object on the map for the whole patrol). A wander-random
anchor is a different thing: a fixed (mapId, x, y, z) captured at the instant
the bot enters RPG_WANDER_RANDOM. There is no WorldObject to point at - only a
position, which must be copied, not referenced (the bot itself is the closest
"object" to the spot, and it moves away from it by design). Repurposing
`center` to serve both would mean this patch's fix quietly overloads the
existing OutdoorPvP call site with the leash logic below, or forces a
WorldObject to be spawned/tracked just to hold a static point. Both are worse
than the alternative taken here: two new, independently-defaulted parameters
(`homeAnchor`, `maxHomeDist`) on `MoveRandomNear`, so every existing caller
(quest wander, grind-spot idle shuffle, outdoor-pvp patrol) is byte-for-byte
unaffected, and `center` is left exactly as inert as it already was - a
separate, pre-existing gap outside this patch's scope.

WHAT THE ANCHOR IS AND WHERE IT IS SET

`NewRpgInfo::WanderRandom` (NewRpgInfo.h:38-45) gains one field:

    WorldPosition anchor{};

set once, in `NewRpgInfo::ChangeToWanderRandom(WorldPosition anchor)`
(NewRpgInfo.cpp:29), which now takes the anchor as a required argument instead
of taking nothing. All three call sites pass `WorldPosition(bot)` - the bot's
own position at the moment it enters the state:

  * NewRpgAction.cpp:103 (explicit "wander random" command)
  * NewRpgAction.cpp:248 (GO_GRIND -> WANDER_RANDOM transition, once the bot
    has arrived at its grind spot)
  * NewRpgBaseAction.cpp:1115 (the weighted-random status roll)

"Where the bot was standing when wandering began" was chosen over "the bot's
last non-wander anchor point" because it needs no new bookkeeping beyond the
state transition itself - anywhere `ChangeToWanderRandom` is called already
has `bot` in scope - and it matches how every other anchored RPG state in this
same file already works (GO_GRIND/GO_CAMP carry a `WorldPosition pos`,
TravelFlight carries `flightMasterPos`, WanderNpc/DoQuest carry a target).
WANDER_RANDOM was the only state in the variant with nothing to anchor to.

The anchor is set ONCE per state entry and deliberately never refreshed inside
`NewRpgWanderRandomAction::Execute()` - refreshing it every tick would just
re-center the leash on wherever drift had already carried the bot, which
defeats the whole point.

WHAT `MoveRandomNear` DOES WITH IT

Two new, defaulted parameters:

    bool MoveRandomNear(float moveStep = 50.0f,
                         MovementPriority priority = MovementPriority::MOVEMENT_NORMAL,
                         WorldObject* center = nullptr,
                         Position const* homeAnchor = nullptr,
                         float maxHomeDist = 0.0f);

When `homeAnchor` is null (every existing caller), behaviour is unchanged.
When it is supplied (only `NewRpgWanderRandomAction::Execute`, with
`maxDriftFromAnchor = 100.0f`):

  * A candidate step that would land further than `maxHomeDist` from the
    anchor is rejected and re-rolled (up to the existing 8 attempts) - this
    alone is what stops normal wander from drifting out in the first place.
  * If the bot is ALREADY at or past `maxHomeDist` (patch installed onto a
    family already mid-drift, or dropped there by a GM/death/whatever), the
    call switches to a "leash" mode: instead of a uniformly random angle each
    attempt, the angle is biased back toward the anchor within a 54-degree
    cone (matching the existing "leading" cone pattern already used in this
    file, e.g. `MoveNearRpgTarget`), so the bot reliably closes distance
    instead of continuing to sample directions that could point further away.

100 yards was chosen as comfortably smaller than the walkable width of any
leveling zone's danger boundary, while still large enough that a wandering
bot visibly moves around rather than pacing a tiny circle.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX (DELIBERATELY OUT OF SCOPE)

`GrindTargetValue::FindTargetForGrinding()` (GrindTargetValue.cpp) early-
returns for anything already in the bot's `attackers` list before its
level-diff and elite checks run, so level/elite filtering is bypassed once a
bot is already engaged. That is a real, separate gap - the existing flee
backstop (infra#2928) already partially covers "already fighting something
dangerous" - but it is not this patch's target and is not touched here. This
patch is scoped to the ROOT CAUSE of the reported incident: the unbounded
random walk that put an under-20 family in Burning Steppes with nothing
chasing them at all.

WHAT WOULD LET THIS PATCH BE DELETED

Upstream giving `RPG_WANDER_RANDOM` (or `MoveRandomNear` generally) its own
zone-safety or leash-distance bound, in whatever shape upstream prefers. Watch
for changes to NewRpgBaseAction::MoveRandomNear or NewRpgWanderRandomAction::
Execute in mod-playerbots; if either grows an equivalent bound, delete this
patch and drop straight back to upstream's version. No upstream PR is open yet
for this fix.

